  "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be the event that the chosen marble is red, and let $A$ be the event that the marble was selected from box $A.$ Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the marble was selected from box $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marble is red, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the marbles are in box $A$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ are in box $B$, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{7}{10}$ of the marbles in both boxes are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|{A}^{\u2032})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{10} & =(\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{3}{4})+P(R|{A}^{\u2032})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{7}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}P(R|{A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}P(R|{A}^{\u2032}) & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(R|{A}^{\u2032}) & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected marble from box $B$ is red is $\\frac{4}{5}.$",
